course: dictionary
lesson: 9151
title: Snoop: looking where you should not
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To snoop means to look secretly around a place, or into someone's private things, to find out about them. A person who does this is also called a snoop, which means someone who is nosy.
explain: Look at the clue in this sentence: «Listening in on someone else's phone call is snooping, even inside your own home.» The words someone else's tell you the call is not yours, so listening to it is poking into something private. Being at home does not make it allowed.
explain: Snooping can hurt people. «A diary is private; using a secret found by snooping to embarrass someone can wreck trust for a long time.» Asking is honest, but «snooping is taking what was not given.»
fact: Snoop can be a verb (to snoop) or a noun (a snoop).
fact: «Snoop around» and «snoop through» mean looking about or searching through things that are not yours.
fact: «Snoop on someone» is informal and means to spy on them or watch what they do.
fact: In stories, detectives and nosy neighbours snoop to find clues.
fact: In everyday life, snooping is seen as rude, because it means taking what is private without being given it.
exercise: «Listening in on someone else's phone call is snooping, even inside your own home.» What is the snooping in this sentence? | *Listening in on someone else's private phone call | Making a cup of tea in your own kitchen | Tidying your own bedroom | Asking a friend a question out loud
exercise: In a story, a detective searches a suspect's drawers when nobody is looking. What is the detective doing? | *Snooping around for clues | Sleeping soundly in a chair | Baking a cake for the team | Running a race in the park
exercise: What does «snoop on someone» mean? | To give someone a present | To thank someone for their help | *To spy on them or watch what they do | To invite someone to a party
test: Which word means someone who is nosy and looks into other people's private things? | *Snoop | Helper | Dreamer | Teacher
test: According to the lessons, which choice is honest? | Watching someone type their code so you can use it later | Reading a secret you found in a friend's diary | *Asking politely for what you need | Looking through drawers when no one is watching
exam: Why can snooping be harmful, according to «A diary is private; using a secret found by snooping to embarrass someone can wreck trust for a long time»? | Because diaries are always expensive | Because it makes you hungry | Because it means asking politely | *Because using a secret found by snooping to embarrass someone can wreck trust for a long time
